From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sat, 8 Aug 2026 12:28:58 +0200
Subject: Strip ordinary aliases when the macro-expansion check reveals opaques
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The macro-expansion conformance check (upstream #25756) dealises opaque types
so aliases invisible at the expansion site do not fail the check, but its
TypeMap only reveals an opaque that heads the type directly. An opaque hidden
behind an ordinary alias (`type Date = Timestamp {...}` over an opaque
`Timestamp`) is left unrevealed — and because the two sides of the check
arrive differently normalized (the expected type from the inline definition's
TASTy is already dealiased; the actual type from the unpickled expansion is
not), well-typed expansions are rejected. Stripping ordinary aliases first
(`tp.dealias`) normalizes both sides identically; `dealias` respects opaque
scopes, so nothing is revealed that the check would not reveal anyway.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude); two-file reproduction in the feature
documentation.

Proscala-Patch: macroalias
---
 compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala | 11 ++++++++---
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
index 51016f50deafdd3ff366d7a7061c675d737a5f58..277121a7a2adf7f05ad71e4e492bd078ee921422 100644
--- a/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
+++ b/compiler/src/dotty/tools/dotc/inlines/Inliner.scala
@@ -1087,13 +1087,18 @@ class Inliner(val call: tpd.Tree)(using Context):
 
           // We dealias opaque types because their aliases might not be visible
           // at the expansion site. See `tests/run-macros/opaque-inline`.
+          // Ordinary aliases are stripped first (`tp.dealias`): an opaque hidden
+          // behind a plain alias (`type Date = Timestamp {...}` over an opaque
+          // `Timestamp`) must be revealed the same way on both sides, or the
+          // conformance check below compares an opaque-revealed expected type
+          // against an unrevealed actual type and fails on well-typed expansions.
           val dealiasOpaques = new TypeMap:
-            def apply(tp: Type): Type = tp match
+            def apply(tp: Type): Type = tp.dealias match
               case tp: TypeRef if tp.typeSymbol.isOpaqueAlias =>
                 val sym = tp.typeSymbol
                 apply(sym.opaqueAlias.asSeenFrom(tp.prefix, sym.owner))
-              case _ =>
-                mapOver(tp)
+              case dealiased =>
+                mapOver(dealiased)
 
           val actualTp = dealiasOpaques(res.tpe)
           val expectedTp = dealiasOpaques(tree1.tpe)
